# inertia & keyframes

`inertia` and `keyframes` are the animation generators behind `transition.type: 'inertia'` and the default tween. They sit next to [`spring`](/docs/api-reference), re-exported unchanged from Motion.

<Example isSmall exampleUrl="/examples/inertia">
  <Default />
</Example>

## inertia

Inertia decelerates a value from its initial velocity. The value coasts toward `origin + power × velocity`. If `min` or `max` is set and the value would cross it, a spring brings it back to the boundary.

Use the string or the function as `type`. Both give the same result:

```ts
import { animate, inertia, motionValue } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

const x = motionValue(0)

animate(x, [0], { type: 'inertia', velocity: 1000, power: 0.5 }) // settles at 500
animate(x, [0], { type: inertia, velocity: 1000, power: 0.5 }) // same
```

### Options

| Option | Default | Description |
|---|---:|---|
| `velocity` | `0` | Initial velocity, in units per second. |
| `power` | `0.8` | How far the value travels: the target is `origin + power × velocity`. |
| `timeConstant` | `325` | Deceleration time constant, in ms. Higher values feel heavier. |
| `min` | none | Lower boundary. Crossing it hands off to the boundary spring. |
| `max` | none | Upper boundary. Crossing it hands off to the boundary spring. |
| `bounceStiffness` | `500` | Stiffness of the boundary spring. |
| `bounceDamping` | `10` | Damping of the boundary spring. |
| `modifyTarget` | none | Adjust the calculated target, e.g. to snap to a grid. |
| `restDelta` | `0.5` | Distance from the target under which the animation can finish. |
| `restSpeed` | spring default | Speed under which the boundary spring can finish. Passed through to it; unset, the spring uses `2`, or `0.01` when the remaining distance is under `5` (motion-dom `generators/inertia.ts`, `generators/spring.ts` @ v14.7.1). |

Snap the resting position to a 50px grid:

```ts
animate(x, [0], {
  type: inertia,
  velocity: 800,
  modifyTarget: (target) => Math.round(target / 50) * 50
})
```

### Sampling a generator by hand

A generator is a function of options that returns `{ next(ms) }`. Call `next` with the elapsed time in milliseconds to read the value without running an animation:

```ts
import { inertia } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

const g = inertia({ keyframes: [0], velocity: 1000 })
g.next(16) // { value: …, done: false }
g.next(5000) // { value: 800, done: true }
```

## keyframes

`keyframes` is the tween generator behind `type: 'keyframes'` / `'tween'`, and the engine's default. It interpolates through the keyframes over `duration`.

| Option | Default | Description |
|---|---:|---|
| `duration` | `300` | Length of the tween in **milliseconds** when sampled directly. (`transition.duration` stays in seconds.) |
| `ease` | `'easeInOut'` | An easing name, function, cubic-bezier array, or one easing per segment. |
| `times` | evenly spaced | Progress (`0`–`1`) of each keyframe. |

```ts
import { keyframes } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

const tween = keyframes({ keyframes: [0, 100], duration: 1000, ease: 'linear' })
tween.next(250).value // 25
```

`spring` works the same way: pass it as `type`, or call it with options and sample `next(ms)`.
